    {
      "id": "testimony.nycc.commercial-rent-stabilization-stated.2022-03-24.speaker.council-member-rivera",
      "displayName": "COUNCIL MEMBER RIVERA",
      "transcriptLabels": [
        "COUNCIL MEMBER RIVERA"
      ],
      "speakerType": "council-member",
      "position": "supportive",
      "classificationBasis": "Rivera says she proudly joins the reintroduction and argues for commercial-rent regulation as part of an equitable recovery.",
      "fullTextIncluded": true,
      "turnCount": 3,
      "wordCount": 372,
      "pageRanges": [
        "7",
        "30",
        "62-64"
      ],
      "turns": [
        {
          "ordinal": 1,
          "pageStart": 7,
          "pageEnd": 7,
          "text": "Present."
        },
        {
          "ordinal": 2,
          "pageStart": 30,
          "pageEnd": 30,
          "text": "I vote aye."
        },
        {
          "ordinal": 3,
          "pageStart": 62,
          "pageEnd": 64,
          "text": "Thank you so much. Today, I proudly join my colleagues Ayala, Cabán, Hudson to introduce-- well, reintroduce commercial rent stabilization because I know this Council can be the one to finally get it done. In the last few years we have seen so many beloved small NEW YORK CITY COUNCIL STATED MEETING 63 businesses in my district and throughout the City close due in large part to rent from gym spas, to Odessa’s [sic], to so many others still open, but eager for these protections like Gatarella [sic] and Dokam [sp?]. We have had a rent problem since long before the pandemic, leaving so many of our small businesses owners scrambling to keep up. This city has had commercial rent regulations in the past, and its past time we do it again. If not now, in support of a just and equitable pandemic recovery, then when? It is time to get this done to ensure long-term stability for the small business community. I’m also introducing legislation calling on the New York State Legislature to pass and the Governor to sign life-saving maternal health legislation carried by our state colleagues. The first is in support of Assembly Member Dick Gottfried and State Senator Gustavo Rivera’s Beyond the Fourth Trimester Bill which would expand Medicaid coverage for new members from 60 days after giving birth to a full year. The second supports Assembly Member Amy Paulin and State Senator Julia Salazar requiring doctors to provide those who are expecting with comprehensive information on Caesarian sections and potential risks NEW YORK CITY COUNCIL STATED MEETING 64 associated with the procedure. I’m pleased to see the Mayor responding to our advocacy around supporting New York City doulas and midwives, but we must do more and we will. This Council is historic. We’re the most diverse with the most women Council Members in history and with the most mothers. We are in a position to pass a policy agenda furthering gender and reproductive justice in New York City, and that agenda must and will address the maternal mortality and morbidity crisis. I urge my colleagues to join me in the fight for birth justice and to sign on to these resolutions. Thank you."
        }
      ],
      "attributionNotes": []
    },
